Yes, exactly. The adb shell command opens a command-line interface directly inside your Android device's operating system.
Because Android is built on top of the Linux kernel, running adb shell gives you access to a standard Linux terminal environment running on your phone.

What you can interact with via adb shell

• File System: You can create, move, view, and permanently delete files (like your APKs) using commands like ls, cd, mkdir, and rm.
• Android Package Manager (pm): You can manually install, uninstall, or list apps directly on the phone.
• Android Activity Manager (am): You can force-stop apps, start background services, or launch specific app screens.
• System Settings (settings): You can change phone settings (like brightness, volume, or developer options) via text commands.
In short, you are not just controlling the phone from the outside; you are remotely logging into the phone's internal operating system to command it directly.
